[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, this is the dashboard and it\'s on whether my laptop is up and running or not. So, the center part of it is the brain. The hexagon in the middle is Cloud .md file. That\'s the top file that tells Cloud where everything lives, and'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around it there are rings such as skills, memory, routines, and applications. Every dot represents one real file in my workspace, a node, a skill, a routine, and rings are my areas'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of interest, such as business, clients, career, etc. We can make it full screen, and there are six different views of the same files. So, the default one is'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rings, the one we are looking at right now. Also, there is circle, areas, links, which is the one I use most, because you can see which skill touches which files, and which routine'

[01:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'writes what. We have timeline, which helps me to track every routine and every file change by day. And of course, the 3D orbit, which is a cool 3D animation of all the files and'

[01:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'relationships between them. Let\'s go to links. Let\'s select business only. And you can see that everything becomes dimmer and just the business files, routines, and skills get selected and highlighted. We'

[02:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also have search functionality, which is super cool. So, let\'s type studio, for example. And there is a skill from my last video, the one which replaces my image and video subscriptions. Let\'s press on'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'open. And the actual file is right here. Well, there is no need to use finder or a terminal. That\'s the whole point of the map. You can find and open any file about your work in just a couple of'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clicks. Let\'s get back to the main dashboard. So, on the left-hand side, we have today, the next 14 days, and the business pulse at the bottom. Every one of them is written by a routine and automated schedule task. And on the'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right-hand side, we have needs path section. That\'s the only list in the whole system that waits for a human. And that\'s the rule I set. So, a plot never marks a task done. Only I tick it. Below'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it, we have the routines board that runs today and their status. As you can see, everything is in green, which is cool. And the whole page, actually, what you see here is just a window. So, nothing on it is stored here. So, it\'s all read'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from the files only. And now, let\'s have a look at what\'s actually behind the page because the dashboard was the last thing I built. I came up with a framework and I call it maps. Essentially, it has four layers and it stands for memory, agent, pulse,'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and screen. Memory is one map of everything I work on. Bot can find any facts without me pointing at it. Agent, which essentially is the same cloud code on two machines, my laptop and a box'

[03:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that never sleeps on a VPS, which is um virtual private server on Hostinger. Then there is pulse, the routines that run when I\'m not at my desk, or meaning while my laptop is shut down or asleep.'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And screen, which essentially is the dashboard we just played with, which shows and never stores the data it shows. Let\'s have a look at them in exactly that order, because that\'s the order I built them. So, there are three'

[04:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'questions to ask about your own setup before we start. First of all, can Claude code actually find any fact in just two hops, like without you pointing at the file? Then, does anything in your setup run while your laptop is closed?'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And does your dashboard only shows things, or it actually has actually became where the truth lives. By the way, for each of the four layers, there\'s a ready-made prompt in the free guide link in the description. You just'

[04:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'paste it into Claude code, and it walks you through building that layer. So, let\'s talk about the memory first. And by memory, I don\'t mean a chat history. What I mean here is whether Claude can actually find the right files without me'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'being involved. Level one is a essentially where most of us start, and it\'s just a pile of folders. Claude can read like 20 files to answer one simple question. And level two is a signpost'

[04:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'files instead of folders. This is the idea I picked up from a fellow YouTube creator, J E, in his Agnostic OS video a few weeks ago. So, please check it out his channel. He\'s brilliant. And uh'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'level three is essentially a generated map. This is where mine sits. Well, and if you\'re starting from nothing, don\'t just write the map yourself. Have Claude or any other LLM, such as Astra 6, interview you for say 20 minutes, one'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'question at a time. So, we just need to provide uh some details on your business, what your business does, what do you do week on week, who do you work with, where do your files live, and it'

[05:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'writes the first draft on the map from from your answers. Actually, I\'m going to provide the interview prompt for you in the description below, so it\'ll be easier for you to set up. As you remember, at the very core of our system'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lives Claude.md file. This is the file. And uh it\'s a very short file, just 40 lines, and it holds no facts at all. It only says where things live, one line per area of my work, like business,'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'career, clients, etc. Each of these lines points to the right file for that area, and that file points to the real documents. From the top file, essentially just two steps away to any'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'documents about my work. And that\'s what you just saw with the um Studio skill example I showed a minute ago, where I could search and fly to an open file in just a couple of clicks. And this is how cloud.md file actually looks for me'

[06:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right now. As you can see, very basic, so there are areas of interest, there is rules and model routing, and some pointers. Just 40 lines of it, and some description about who I am, what I do. It\'s really short, as you can tell. I\'ve'

[06:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'set up two simple rules here to follow. So, the first one is every fact has exactly one home, so nothing gets written twice. And the second one is a small check that runs nightly and flags'

[06:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'any signpost that points to a file that has moved. So, the map can\'t go stale. Now, let\'s move to the agent, and this is where mine is a bit unusual. At least I haven\'t seen anyone else doing it yet.'

[07:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s the same cloud code subscription you already have, or OpenAI subscription, but running on two machines. One on your own laptop or PC, it doesn\'t really matter, and one on the small computer I rent in the cloud. In'

[07:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this case, it\'s Hostinger VPS. VPS stands for virtual private server. Essentially, what it is, it\'s a small computer which lives on a cloud, and it never sleeps. Both machines hold a copy of the same folder, and it syncs itself'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'every 5 minutes in both directions. Whichever cloud it\'s running on, it has the same map and the same skills with that the phone. So, I send the voice note to a chatbot on the server, and what happens next, it turns my voice'

[07:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into text, like works out what I\'m asking for, and then Claude answers to me in the same chat in Telegram. Okay, let\'s demonstrate it now and see how it works in practice. Let\'s send a voice message to our chatbot. What\'s on today?'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So we can see it\'s listening. It\'s going to transcribe our message into text, send it to Claude, and Claude comes back with his response. It worked exactly as we expected. So there are two things'

[08:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that really matter here because this server works while I\'m asleep, as we already learned, and nobody\'s watching it. First of all, it\'s really great from the privacy perspective because it\'s locked away from the public internet. I use a free tool called Tailscale, which'

[08:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'puts my laptop, my phone, and the server on their own private network. There is no public address anyone can even knock on. Only my own devices can see it. And second is that Claude on that server is quite limited, and it\'s done on purpose.'

[08:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There is just one settings file which blocks every tool that could send, share, or delete anything. It can write an email for me, for example, but it cannot send one. So I tried to talk it into sending one, and it refused. And'

[08:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s the expected behavior, of course. The worst it can do is to actually leave me a draft. On the ladder, we have level one, where Claude code lives on your own laptop or PC. Level two adds the box that never sleeps. And level three is'

[08:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the same folder living on both. Quick word about today\'s sponsor, which is me. I run Automation Orbit, and one of the things we build for businesses is an AI Search Visibility Audit. A check of how visible your business is in AI'

[09:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Search. Customers ask ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, or Claude before they ask Google now. And the audit shows whether you come up in those answers or not, and what to fix so you do. So we run it on a number of clients already,'

[09:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the first thing we did, we run it on our own website, which scored 47. We fixed the issues the same day, and re-audited at 62. So if you want that for your business, the link in the description. Right, back to the video.'

[09:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, where are we right now? Okay, Claude has a map and it\'s leaves on two machines, but my first plan was to run Hermes on that server, the open-source agent, and there are plenty of YouTube videos on how to set it up, but'

[09:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Anthropic\'s rules don\'t let you use your own Claude subscription through someone else\'s app or harness. What you can do though is to run Claude code itself on a server, sign in with your own account. That\'s exactly what I do and costs me'

[10:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nothing extra. If you are using OpenAI, for example, their terms might be different, so they might allow you to use third-party agents like Hermes. Just check it out before you make this decision. Let\'s have a quick look at the'

[10:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'routines now. So, I have this file, which is routines.yaml, and it\'s essentially a list all the routines I have, their purpose, when they are scheduled, and which machine going to run them. A scheduler on each'

[10:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'machine actually checks that list every 5 minutes and runs whatever is due. Every run leaves a record, which is what the board on the dashboard reads, and there is a cap, so everything that keeps failing for some reason can\'t burn'

[10:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through the whole month overnight. Now, let\'s have a quick look at the skills deck. The cleanup skill, which I use quite a lot, frees up the memory on my Mac and closes the leftover processes that slow'

[10:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it down. Brain build rebuilds the brain you saw in the center every night, and vault lint checks my notes for broken ones. Talking about routines, and the one I use the most is the morning'

[11:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'digest. It lands in my calendar before I\'m awake, and it\'s a summary of what has happened overnight, what is due today, and who is waiting on me. This really helps me to self-organize and prepare for the working day. On the'

[11:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ladder, level one is running things by hand, level two is scheduled tasks on your laptop, which stop working the moment you close it, and level three is fully automated scheduled tasks, which run without you.'

[11:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Last layer, the screen, and here\'s the rule I\'d keep even if you never built a dashboard before. Show, but don\'t store. Every panel on this page is read from a file. That\'s something else already created or wrote. All the panels on the'

[11:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'left-hand side are built on my laptop because that\'s where my tracker leaves, and the routines board on the right-hand side actually are on the server side because that\'s where the routines run.'

[11:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The brain is rebuilt every night from my notes. So, the page itself stores nothing. It\'s only shows, and that\'s why it\'s safe to look at from my phone on a holiday, for example. If a dashboard died tomorrow, I\'d lose nothing. Every fact sits in a file I own. Only a letter'

[12:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'level one is just a pretty graph you just look at. Level two is the panels that read your files, and level three is what you see here, a fully functional dashboard you can press buttons on, and'

[12:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things start happening on the right machine. And if you want similar views like rings, links, the 3D orbit, I\'ve attached the prompt for those here, so it\'s going to be in card five in the guide.'

[12:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I wanted to show you that the maps framework actually works, and that\'s why I decided to measure it. So, it\'s not just me saying it works. I asked the same question twice, "Which words am I not allowed to use in a LinkedIn post, and where is that written down?" What'

[12:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m asking here, I\'m asking Claude actually to find the real file of mine which contains a list of banned words that make a post sound like an AI slop. On the left-hand side, we have a fresh Claude session in my workspace with no'

[13:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'map, and on the right-hand side, we have the same question, but this time Claude starts from the map. Both found the right file, but look at the time. So, without the map, it spent 1 minute and 2 seconds, while with the map, it spent'

[13:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just 30 seconds, half of the time. Then, I type context on both, which shows how many tokens have been spent on that question. Look at the message line. So, that\'s the reading and writing for this'

[13:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one question. On left-hand side, we have 28,000 tokens used and on the right-hand side, just 17,000 tokens, about 40% less. I ran each side twice and the second time the gap in reading was'

[13:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'smaller, but the map was still about twice as fast. This confirms that the map isn\'t a tidiness thing, it\'s the difference between Claude searching my whole workspace and going straight to the right file. When you don\'t have a'

[13:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'maps enabled, you essentially pay for that twice in money and waiting. I was away for 10 days in August. My laptop was closed, but I had everything I needed on my mobile. All the routines kept running, the digest landed every'

[14:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'morning, and when I got back, I didn\'t have to rebuild a single thing in my hat. That\'s what an operating system is for. It isn\'t a graph or a pretty dashboard. Honestly, that\'s just 20% of it. It\'s a place that remembers your'

[14:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'business, runs while you\'re away, and only asks you when a human is needed. And the coolest part, it leaves in plain files, the files you own, that can be read by any LLM model like Claude, OpenAI models, or a cheaper open-source'

[14:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one like Deep Seek, for example. If you decide to build your own thing from this video, I\'d highly suggest to start from map first, and then the screen can wait. So, if you want to build your own'

[14:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'version, the guide is in the description and it\'s free. Next, I\'m planning to cover version two with more functionality in it. If you enjoyed the video, please smash like button, subscribe to the channel, it\'s going to help me a lot, and I\'ll see you in the'

[14:53] [scene:talk]
